{#
  The HUB side, rendered on k3s_edge only.

  One [Peer] per home node. There is deliberately no single home-side
  terminator: every node dials the hub directly, so there is nothing to fail
  over -- a dead node is an ordinary dead node and Kubernetes already handles
  that.

  ⚠️ No Endpoint on any peer. Home is behind NAT and always initiates; the hub
  learns each peer's address from its first handshake. That is also why the
  MikroTik needs no configuration at all and no inbound port is opened at home.
#}
# Managed by Ansible -- ansible/roles/wireguard. Do not edit on the node.
#
# Rendered from the whole k3s_nodes group. A --limit run would write a peer
# list with HOLES, and a node missing from AllowedIPs is dropped SILENTLY --
# no log, no counter, no error. The role refuses to run limited.

[Interface]
Address = {{ wg_address }}/24
ListenPort = {{ wg_port }}
MTU = {{ wg_mtu }}
PostUp = wg set %i private-key {{ wg_private_key_path }}
{% for host in groups['k3s_nodes'] | difference(groups['k3s_edge']) | sort %}

# {{ host }} -- {{ hostvars[host].k8s_node_name }}
[Peer]
PublicKey = {{ hostvars[host].wg_pubkey }}
{#
  BOTH /32s, deliberately.

  The node keeps its 192.168.33.x InternalIP (changing that is a seven-node
  rejoin), so Cilium's VXLAN from here is addressed to the LAN address and it
  must be permitted. But the SOURCE address a home node uses when IT initiates
  is chosen by the kernel and depends on how Cilium binds its tunnel socket --
  which could be either address.

  Listing both makes the answer not matter. It cannot cause cross-peer
  confusion because both belong to this one peer, and it costs nothing.

  ⚠️ {{ wg_client_subnet }} (the NetBird client range) appears NOWHERE here,
  and must not be added. Clients are SNAT'd at the hub to {{ wg_address }}
  before their traffic enters the cluster, so no node ever sees such a source.
  Adding it is a no-op that reads like a fix.
#}
AllowedIPs = {{ hostvars[host].ansible_host }}/32, {{ hostvars[host].wg_address }}/32
{% endfor %}
